1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,850
Lien Ying Chow, the Banker
Who Escaped to Chongqing

2
00:00:03,850 --> 00:00:09,903
In the last days before Singapore
surrendered to the Japanese in February 1942,

3
00:00:09,983 --> 00:00:13,629
a Blue Funnel cargo liner
called the Gorgon left

4
00:00:13,709 --> 00:00:18,600
the harbour for Australia with
more than 350 refugees on board.

5
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:22,789
One of them was Lien Ying Chow,
the 35-year-old president of

6
00:00:22,869 --> 00:00:26,987
the Singapore Chinese Chamber
of Commerce, who was carrying

7
00:00:27,067 --> 00:00:31,550
some cash and two diamonds hidden
in the lining of his clothes.

8
00:00:31,550 --> 00:00:34,979
The ship survived a Japanese
dive-bombing attack on

9
00:00:35,059 --> 00:00:38,775
the way and reached Fremantle
on the 20th of February.

10
00:00:38,775 --> 00:00:43,790
Seven years later, Lien opened the
first new bank in postwar Singapore,

11
00:00:43,870 --> 00:00:46,301
Overseas Union Bank, and for half a

12
00:00:46,381 --> 00:00:50,400
century it was one of the
island's four big local banks.

13
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:55,155
Lien was born on the 2nd of August
1906 in the village of Dapu,

14
00:00:55,235 --> 00:01:00,300
in Guangdong province, where his
grandfather was the village head.

15
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:02,350
He was Teochew.

16
00:01:02,350 --> 00:01:07,822
His mother died in 1916 and his father
died of plague the following year,

17
00:01:07,902 --> 00:01:11,400
and Lien went to live
with a great-granduncle.

18
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:15,940
In 1918 he moved to Hong Kong,
where he worked for two years,

19
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:19,878
and in 1920, with the help
of a relative and savings

20
00:01:19,958 --> 00:01:24,275
of 10 Hong Kong dollars, he
bought a ticket to Singapore.

21
00:01:24,275 --> 00:01:27,750
According to the National
Library Board's account,

22
00:01:27,830 --> 00:01:32,087
which is based on his 1992
memoir, he arrived with a singlet,

23
00:01:32,167 --> 00:01:35,856
a pair of short trousers
and a samfu, the loose tunic

24
00:01:35,936 --> 00:01:39,775
and trousers worn by working
people in southern China.

25
00:01:39,775 --> 00:01:44,531
A family friend found him a job
as an assistant at Kian Thye,

26
00:01:44,611 --> 00:01:48,575
a ship chandler on Robinson
Road, for $10 a month.

27
00:01:48,575 --> 00:01:53,175
A ship chandler supplies ships
with provisions and equipment.

28
00:01:53,175 --> 00:01:56,859
The owner, Tay Woo Seng,
took him under his wing

29
00:01:56,939 --> 00:02:00,545
and made him an assistant
shipping clerk at 16,

30
00:02:00,625 --> 00:02:04,625
and Lien rose to assistant
manager on $120 a month.

31
00:02:04,625 --> 00:02:09,177
He left in 1927 with $5,000
in savings and started

32
00:02:09,257 --> 00:02:14,075
his own chandlery, which
he closed after six months.

33
00:02:14,075 --> 00:02:18,308
In 1929 he tried again with
a partner, How Wan Hong,

34
00:02:18,388 --> 00:02:22,950
and formed Wah Hin & Co., in
which he held 60 per cent.

35
00:02:22,950 --> 00:02:27,028
Wah Hin supplied provisions
to the British armed forces,

36
00:02:27,108 --> 00:02:32,745
first in Singapore and then in Penang,
Kuala Lumpur and Ipoh, and by the late

37
00:02:32,825 --> 00:02:37,800
1930s Lien was one of the most
successful businessmen in Singapore.

38
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:42,094
In 1941, at 34, he became
the youngest president

39
00:02:42,174 --> 00:02:46,275
of the Singapore Chinese
Chamber of Commerce.

40
00:02:46,275 --> 00:02:51,268
As Chamber president, Lien helped
to organise relief work when

41
00:02:51,348 --> 00:02:56,175
Japanese aircraft began bombing
Singapore in December 1941.

42
00:02:56,175 --> 00:03:00,923
As the Japanese army advanced down
Malaya and across the Johor Strait,

43
00:03:01,003 --> 00:03:05,199
he left on the Gorgon, one of
the ships that carried civilians

44
00:03:05,279 --> 00:03:09,900
out in the final week before the
surrender on the 15th of February.

45
00:03:09,900 --> 00:03:13,759
Its passenger list on
arrival in Fremantle,

46
00:03:13,839 --> 00:03:16,873
on the coast of Western Australia,

47
00:03:16,953 --> 00:03:21,350
names 357 people, 56 of
them with Chinese names.

48
00:03:21,350 --> 00:03:24,244
From Australia, Lien
made his way to China,

49
00:03:24,324 --> 00:03:28,325
where his family was, and to
Chongqing, the wartime capital

50
00:03:28,405 --> 00:03:31,725
of the Chinese government
under Chiang Kai-shek.

51
00:03:31,725 --> 00:03:34,858
Chongqing lay far up the Yangtze River,

52
00:03:34,938 --> 00:03:39,965
beyond the reach of the Japanese
army but not of its aircraft,

53
00:03:40,045 --> 00:03:44,575
which bombed the city repeatedly
between 1938 and 1943.

54
00:03:44,575 --> 00:03:48,245
Lien met Chiang and was
appointed to the government's

55
00:03:48,325 --> 00:03:51,853
wartime political council
to represent the overseas

56
00:03:51,933 --> 00:03:55,400
Chinese of Singapore,
Malaya and South-East Asia.

57
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:58,250
He also went back into business.

58
00:03:58,250 --> 00:04:02,809
In 1943 he gathered business
leaders from Singapore, Malaya,

59
00:04:02,889 --> 00:04:07,913
Burma and India who were also in
Chongqing and formed the Overseas

60
00:04:07,993 --> 00:04:12,400
Chinese Union Bank, with Lien
as its executive president.

61
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:15,758
Many of its customers
were Chinese who had fled

62
00:04:15,838 --> 00:04:19,350
the war in South-East
Asia, as its founders had.

63
00:04:19,350 --> 00:04:22,950
The bank opened several branches in China.

64
00:04:22,950 --> 00:04:27,420
After the war, civil conflict and
runaway inflation made banking

65
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:32,050
in China very difficult, and the
bank closed its business there.

66
00:04:32,050 --> 00:04:35,630
Lien returned to Singapore
in October 1945 and

67
00:04:35,710 --> 00:04:39,847
worked with the British
Military Administration as an

68
00:04:39,927 --> 00:04:43,825
adviser on the economy,
food supplies and relief.

69
00:04:43,825 --> 00:04:48,235
In 1947 he began planning
a new bank in Singapore,

70
00:04:48,315 --> 00:04:53,802
and it opened at 9 a.m. on
Saturday, the 5th of February 1949,

71
00:04:53,882 --> 00:04:57,025
in Meyer Chambers on Raffles Place.

72
00:04:57,025 --> 00:05:01,998
The same ground floor had
been the Singapore office of

73
00:05:02,078 --> 00:05:07,225
the Yokohama Specie Bank from
1933 until December 1941.

74
00:05:07,225 --> 00:05:11,200
The new bank was called
Overseas Union Bank.

75
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:15,077
It had $2 million in paid-up
capital and 27 staff,

76
00:05:15,157 --> 00:05:21,725
including Lien and its general manager,
Chi Owyang, who had run the Chongqing bank.

77
00:05:21,725 --> 00:05:25,782
The Straits Times noted the
day before it opened that there

78
00:05:25,862 --> 00:05:30,350
were "more than a dozen
multi-millionaires" on its 15-man board.

79
00:05:30,350 --> 00:05:35,152
The chairman was Tan Sia Kuang, a
banker and rubber company owner,

80
00:05:35,232 --> 00:05:37,525
and Lien was managing director.

81
00:05:37,525 --> 00:05:41,816
The board also included Aw Boon
Haw, the maker of Tiger Balm,

82
00:05:41,896 --> 00:05:46,625
the rubber magnate Tan Lark Sye
and the cinema owner Loke Wan Tho.

83
00:05:46,625 --> 00:05:51,009
Raffles Place had until then
been the preserve of established

84
00:05:51,089 --> 00:05:55,327
European and American banks
and trading houses, and the new

85
00:05:55,407 --> 00:05:59,725
bank paid $4,000 a month for
its ground floor and basement.

86
00:05:59,725 --> 00:06:04,975
More than 1,000 people, mostly
businessmen, came to the opening.

87
00:06:04,975 --> 00:06:09,385
The Sunday Times reported it on
its front page the next day under

88
00:06:09,465 --> 00:06:13,805
the headline "People's Bank
Opened," and said the bank aimed "to

89
00:06:13,885 --> 00:06:18,375
offer all classes of people and
nationalities a uniform service."

90
00:06:18,375 --> 00:06:21,550
Its first niche was the rice trade.

91
00:06:21,550 --> 00:06:26,650
Singapore depended on imported
rice, much of it from Thailand.

92
00:06:26,650 --> 00:06:30,924
The bank built ties with Thai
banks and rice exporters,

93
00:06:31,004 --> 00:06:34,170
and up to the mid-1950s it financed about

94
00:06:34,250 --> 00:06:38,050
80 per cent of the rice
imported into Singapore.

95
00:06:38,050 --> 00:06:41,085
By the end of its third year it had paid

96
00:06:41,165 --> 00:06:44,125
shareholders a dividend of 5 per cent.

97
00:06:44,125 --> 00:06:49,274
From the mid-1950s the bank opened
branches in Malacca, Penang,

98
00:06:49,354 --> 00:06:54,750
Kuala Lumpur and Hong Kong, and
in the 1960s in Tokyo and London.

99
00:06:54,750 --> 00:06:58,882
By 1968 it had 32 branches in Singapore

100
00:06:58,962 --> 00:07:03,175
and deposits of more than $300 million.

101
00:07:03,175 --> 00:07:08,442
In 1973 it became the first
Singapore bank to open a branch

102
00:07:08,522 --> 00:07:14,050
in New York, and in 1975 it was
listed on the stock exchange.

103
00:07:14,050 --> 00:07:19,056
By then it was one of
Singapore's four big local banks,

104
00:07:19,136 --> 00:07:21,725
alongside DBS, OCBC and UOB.

105
00:07:21,725 --> 00:07:25,275
Lien had other ventures beside the bank.

106
00:07:25,275 --> 00:07:27,993
In 1964 he began planning a

107
00:07:28,073 --> 00:07:30,975
large hotel on Orchard Road.

108
00:07:30,975 --> 00:07:34,253
The bank's general manager
did not want the bank

109
00:07:34,333 --> 00:07:37,122
to carry the risk, so a separate company,

110
00:07:37,202 --> 00:07:40,700
Overseas Union Enterprise,
was set up to build it.

111
00:07:40,700 --> 00:07:45,125
The Mandarin Singapore opened in 1971.

112
00:07:45,125 --> 00:07:49,050
The project he followed
longest was a headquarters.

113
00:07:49,050 --> 00:07:53,280
In 1948, a year before the
bank opened, Lien began buying

114
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:57,061
land at 1 Raffles Place,
plot by plot, and it took

115
00:07:57,141 --> 00:08:01,300
him more than three decades
to assemble the whole site.

116
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:03,730
The OUB Centre, a 60-storey,

117
00:08:03,810 --> 00:08:09,379
280-metre tower designed by the
Japanese architect Kenzo Tange,

118
00:08:09,459 --> 00:08:14,041
was completed in 1986 and
officially opened in 1988,

119
00:08:14,121 --> 00:08:18,425
the year Lien was named
Businessman of the Year.

120
00:08:18,425 --> 00:08:23,138
It cost 500 million Singapore
dollars and, according to its owners,

121
00:08:23,218 --> 00:08:27,225
was then the tallest building
outside the United States.

122
00:08:27,225 --> 00:08:31,125
His public roles were as
varied as his business.

123
00:08:31,125 --> 00:08:34,781
Lien served as a municipal
commissioner in the

124
00:08:34,861 --> 00:08:40,059
1950s and as Singapore's High
Commissioner to Malaysia from 1966,

125
00:08:40,139 --> 00:08:42,900
after the two countries separated.

126
00:08:42,900 --> 00:08:47,018
He helped to found Nanyang
University and Ngee Ann College,

127
00:08:47,098 --> 00:08:50,932
now Ngee Ann Polytechnic,
and he was the first chairman

128
00:08:51,012 --> 00:08:54,925
of the council of the National
University of Singapore.

129
00:08:54,925 --> 00:08:58,061
In 1972 he became the first chairman

130
00:08:58,141 --> 00:09:01,625
of the Preservation of Monuments Board.

131
00:09:01,625 --> 00:09:07,000
In 1980 he gave almost half his
wealth to set up the Lien Foundation,

132
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:10,875
a philanthropic foundation
that is still active.

133
00:09:10,875 --> 00:09:14,537
Lien retired as chairman and director

134
00:09:14,617 --> 00:09:17,550
of the bank in 1995, aged 88.

135
00:09:17,550 --> 00:09:22,224
In the 1990s it grew faster than
any of the other three local banks,

136
00:09:22,304 --> 00:09:24,810
but it remained the smallest of them,

137
00:09:24,890 --> 00:09:28,934
and when the local banks came
under pressure to consolidate

138
00:09:29,014 --> 00:09:32,300
in the early 2000s it
became a takeover target.

139
00:09:32,300 --> 00:09:36,106
In June 2001 DBS made a hostile bid

140
00:09:36,186 --> 00:09:39,850
of 9.4 billion Singapore dollars.

141
00:09:39,850 --> 00:09:44,024
UOB answered with a bid of
10 billion Singapore dollars

142
00:09:44,104 --> 00:09:48,123
that had the support of the
bank's board and of Lien,

143
00:09:48,203 --> 00:09:52,225
whose family held about
15.7 per cent of its shares.

144
00:09:52,225 --> 00:09:57,132
In September 2001 UOB won more
than 90 per cent acceptance,

145
00:09:57,212 --> 00:10:01,274
and by the end of 2003
the last branches had been

146
00:10:01,354 --> 00:10:05,750
converted and the name
Overseas Union Bank was gone.

147
00:10:05,750 --> 00:10:10,250
The story of the buyer is
told in the post on UOB.

148
00:10:10,250 --> 00:10:13,719
Lien died on the 6th of August 2004,

149
00:10:13,799 --> 00:10:17,250
a few days after his 98th birthday.

150
00:10:17,250 --> 00:10:21,304
The tower he took decades
to build still stands at

151
00:10:21,384 --> 00:10:25,600
1 Raffles Place, renamed
One Raffles Place in 2012.

152
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:28,660
Lien could carry his savings out of

153
00:10:28,740 --> 00:10:31,700
Singapore in 1942 in his clothes.

154
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:34,587
Most people with money
in a bank could not,

155
00:10:34,667 --> 00:10:38,520
and in a war or a bank failure,
depositors in that period

156
00:10:38,600 --> 00:10:42,050
had no protection beyond
the bank's own soundness.

157
00:10:42,050 --> 00:10:47,070
Today, Singapore has a deposit
insurance scheme, which began in

158
00:10:47,150 --> 00:10:52,250
2006 and is run by the Singapore
Deposit Insurance Corporation.

159
00:10:52,250 --> 00:10:57,686
If a member bank or finance company
fails, each depositor's insured

160
00:10:57,766 --> 00:11:02,707
deposits with it are covered up
to 100,000 Singapore dollars,

161
00:11:02,787 --> 00:11:08,550
a limit raised from 75,000 Singapore
dollars on the 1st of April 2024.

162
00:11:08,550 --> 00:11:12,585
The Monetary Authority of
Singapore said the higher

163
00:11:12,665 --> 00:11:16,700
limit would fully cover
91 per cent of depositors.

164
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:21,074
The scheme does not cover everything,
and amounts above the limit

165
00:11:21,154 --> 00:11:25,322
and some kinds of product are
outside it, but it means that an

166
00:11:25,402 --> 00:11:29,650
ordinary saver no longer depends
on the fate of a single bank.

167
00:11:29,650 --> 00:11:33,085
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Lien Ying Chow is

168
00:11:33,165 --> 00:11:37,005
remembered mainly through the
institutions he left behind,

169
00:11:37,085 --> 00:11:41,275
a tower in Raffles Place and a
foundation that bears his name.

170
00:11:41,275 --> 00:11:45,350
The bank he built has already
disappeared into another.

171
00:11:45,350 --> 00:11:49,783
Its origin is less known: a
refugee bank in wartime Chongqing,

172
00:11:49,863 --> 00:11:52,912
run by people who had
fled South-East Asia,

173
00:11:52,992 --> 00:11:56,850
which became the first new
bank of postwar Singapore.
